var LIMITS_PANEL = {
  id: "limits",
  title: "What burpsy does not do",
  summary: "Every row is a decision, not a defect — and each one is a way a result can be narrower than it looks. Read this before reading a quiet scan as an absent vulnerability.",
  source: "ROADMAP.md § Deliberately out of scope; burpsy-go/ui-gaps.md; each row re-checked against the code",
  rows: [
    {
      term: "Out-of-band BCheck checks",
      detail: "BCheck's interactions operator has no interaction server behind it. The 26 corpus checks that use it are counted into a Collaborator bucket when the corpus loads and are never dispatched.",
      note: "generate_collaborator_address() is deliberately not stubbed: an address nothing polls would convert all 26 into silent false negatives. The catalog's own OOB rows — SSRF, blind SQLi, XXE, the three external-service-interaction issues — do run, but only when the interactsh-client binary is on PATH; without it they decline with a reason instead of reporting clean."
    },
    {
      term: "HTTPS without -intercept-tls",
      detail: "The proxy blind-tunnels CONNECT and records one message-less row with tls: true. The requests inside that tunnel are never in HTTP history, and nothing scans them.",
      note: 'The same is true per host for anything matched by TLS passthrough. /ca.crt answers 404 and the status bar reads "no CA" when interception is off, so the console never offers a certificate it cannot hand out.'
    },
    {
      term: "Parse rate is not run rate",
      detail: "All 264 published BCheck files parse. That number says nothing about how many reached a verdict against your traffic.",
      note: "GET /api/scanner/coverage reports every loaded check as never dispatched, ran clean, ran and found, or errored, with a reason for each skip. With nothing loaded it says so rather than implying a clean scan."
    },
    {
      term: "Loading Burp extensions",
      detail: "Impossible: no JVM, and no binary or source compatibility with MontoyaApi. There is no plugin surface of any kind — the extension phase was never shipped.",
      note: "Of userInterface()'s registration points, the ten Swing ones are out of scope for lack of an honest web analogue. The three declarative ones — context-menu items, message-editor tabs, menu actions — are in scope."
    },
    {
      term: "Deleting captured traffic",
      detail: "The capture store is append-only. It has put, add and annotate, and no delete of any kind; History's filters hide rows and remove nothing."
    },
    {
      term: "The site map only grows",
      detail: "Same store, same property: the site map adds and never removes. A host on the map was observed at some point — that is not evidence it is reachable now."
    },
    {
      term: "Custom scan configurations",
      detail: "Six of Burp's 29 shipped configurations set a key this engine implements, and there are no custom-config knobs, so there is no configuration wizard — it would offer controls that do nothing.",
      note: `The working path is the row context menu's "Audit exchange", or Target → Issues → targeted audit with the config selector.`
    },
    {
      term: "Bambda filters",
      detail: "importBambda takes Java source. The filter substrate here is the closed typed grammar above, deliberately not a scripting engine.",
      note: 'The route answers 400 with a grammar reference — never {"imported": true} for something that was never executed.'
    },
    {
      term: "Project files",
      detail: "Burp's exportProjectFileAndExit, loadProjectFile and restart all presuppose a project file. Durable state here is a capture directory plus a SQLite mirror, which is a different object.",
      note: "project().name() returns null on purpose. A name derived from the -db filename would land in report headers as if an operator had chosen it."
    },
    {
      term: "An IP address column",
      detail: "The exchange record carries no IP field, so History cannot show one. A column filled from a fresh lookup would report where the host resolves now, not where the request went."
    },
    {
      term: "Show response in browser",
      detail: "Absent. Burp loops the response back through its own listener and burpsy has no loopback surface, so the menu item does not exist rather than existing and failing."
    },
    {
      term: "An MCP server",
      detail: "Not shipped, in this port or in the Rust reference. The JSON API is what drives burpsy today; docs/MCP.md records the plan as a plan."
    },
    {
      term: "A desktop shell",
      detail: "A native window ships: cmd/desktop is a Wails v2 shell with a native File/View/Tools/Help menu bar, native dialogs and the same console UI served over a reverse proxy. There is no system tray, and the browser page on the -web address remains the headless path — burpsy stays usable over SSH without the shell."
    },
    {
      term: "Porting Burp's Java scan checks",
      detail: "Out of scope as derivative work. Checks are implemented from the published specs and the public BCheck and Montoya contracts instead — which is also why the corpus is loaded at runtime rather than vendored."
    },
    {
      term: "Enterprise and CI features",
      detail: "Out of scope. Several are Enterprise-only in Burp itself and have no desktop-product behaviour to match."
    }
  ]
};
